              "text": "You are likely referring to **Iuka, Mississippi** (with a capital **I**, which is often confused with a lowercase **L** on screens). \n\nLocated in the far northeastern corner of the state, Iuka is the county seat of Tishomingo County. Although it is a small city with a population of just over 3,100, it is rich in history, unique geography, and outdoor recreation.\n\n### 1. Woodall Mountain (Mississippi's Highest Point)\nJust south of Iuka lies **Woodall Mountain**, which is the highest natural point in Mississippi at **806 feet (246 meters)** above sea level. \n* **Civil War Significance:** While not a massive mountain, it is unique among state high points because it was the site of active Civil War combat. During the Battle of Iuka in September 1862, Union forces used the elevation to launch artillery onto Confederate troops. Because of this, it is sometimes referred to as \"America's Bloodiest High Point\".\n\n### 2. Mineral Springs Park\nNamed after the Chickasaw Chief Iuka, this historic park in the center of town is famous for its natural mineral springs.\n* **The Legend:** According to local legend, Chief Iuka was gravely ill and was carried by his followers to these springs, where drinking the water miraculously restored his health.\n* **World's Fair Winner:** In 1904, water bottled from Iuka's mineral springs won first prize for the purest and best mineral water at the St. Louis World's Fair. Visitors today can still visit the park and drink the water.\n\n### 3. The Battle of Iuka\nOn September 19, 1862, the town was the site of the **Battle of Iuka**, an important early clash in the Corinth Campaign of the American Civil War. Union forces under Major General William Rosecrans successfully stalled a Confederate advance led by Major General Sterling Price. \n* **Acoustic Shadow:** The battle is famous among military historians for a rare atmospheric phenomenon known as an \"acoustic shadow.\" Major General Ulysses S. Grant, who was waiting just a few miles away to launch a coordinated double-attack, never heard the sounds of the battle due to wind patterns and topography, preventing him from sending reinforcements in time.\n\n### 4. \"Marriage Capital of the South\"\nIuka's **Old Tishomingo County Courthouse** (built in 1870) is now a museum, but it holds a fascinating piece of local history. For many decades, Mississippi did not require a three-day waiting period or blood tests for marriage licenses, while neighboring Alabama and Tennessee did. Consequently, couples rushed across the state line to Iuka, leading to the courthouse issuing over 44,000 marriage licenses and earning the town its romantic nickname.\n\n### 5. Nearby Outdoors and Recreation\nBecause of its location in the foothills of the Appalachian Mountains, the area around Iuka looks very different from the flat Mississippi Delta. It is a gateway to several major outdoor destinations:\n* **Pickwick Lake & Tennessee River:** Popular for boating, fishing, and water sports.\n* **J.P. Coleman State Park:** Located just north of town, offering great camping, hiking, and cabins along the water.\n* **Tishomingo State Park:** Located along the historic Natchez Trace Parkway, famous for its massive rock formations and scenic hiking trails.",
